WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.286
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.286 --> 00:00:10.236
My mother started as a first year student
at the University of North Carolina in

00:00:10.236 --> 00:00:10.990
1972.

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:17.310
That would not have been possible had
she been born just a few years earlier.

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:19.050
For most of its history,

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:23.780
UNC was an exclusively male institution,
like many universities.

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:29.770
And while we've come an incredible
long way from there,

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:33.380
sexism is still alive and well.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:35.650
Indeed, it's living in our language.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:40.088
Now, I'm not talking about words that
were designed to denigrate women.

00:00:40.088 --> 00:00:45.000
Bitch, whore, slut.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.820
I'm talking about seemingly innocent
words, words that we hear every day.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:51.898
Perhaps we even speak them.

00:00:51.898 --> 00:00:53.723
Mankind.

00:00:53.723 --> 00:00:56.060
Man made.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:57.940
Or popular here at Stanford, Chairman.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:00:59.635
Fellow.

00:00:59.635 --> 00:01:04.460
Male centered words are everywhere.

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:08.450
We're taught that all
men are created equal.

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:09.752
We man tables and

00:01:09.752 --> 00:01:16.057
use our manpower to elect Congressmen
who rule in man made institutions.

00:01:16.057 --> 00:01:16.831
Man.

00:01:16.831 --> 00:01:21.780
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We laugh, but I'm

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.620
really concerned with these associations
due to our unconscious beliefs.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:32.070
How does the perpetual association of
male terms with power and with strength,

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:37.480
and female terms with insult and
with weakness shape our expectations for

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:42.550
men and for women, our expectations
of our self, or even our self worth?

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:48.590
Children can help us understand
just how powerful language can be.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:52.650
My niece, Ella, is five years old.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.770
She has three older brothers,
and I was babysitting for them.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:59.350
And so I asked them what do
you guys want to do tonight?

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:01.250
Ella was silent.

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:05.850
A little later as I was tucking her
into bed she looked up at me and

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:10.940
she said Uncle Win, how come you only
wanted to know what the guys wanted to do?

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:15.490
Why didn't you care about me?

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:16.830
My heart sank.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:21.840
Of course,
I wanted to hear what she had to say.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:23.040
I love Ella's opinions.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.815
Sometimes, I love Ella the most.

00:02:24.815 --> 00:02:30.400
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I never meant to suggest

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:35.570
that she didn't matter, but that's
exactly what I had done through my words.

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:38.780
And thank goodness she
said something to me.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:42.390
When I had said you guys to her,
she took me literally.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:44.980
And quite logically, she felt left out.

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:50.740
How often had she felt that way before?

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:54.676
How many of you have ever felt that
way before, excluded by language,

00:02:54.676 --> 00:02:58.160
even if you knew that that
wasn't the speaker's intention?

00:03:03.029 --> 00:03:06.420
Male-centered words are everywhere.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.827
When you flip the words around,

00:03:08.827 --> 00:03:13.654
how many men do you know that feel
flattered to be called a woman?

00:03:15.813 --> 00:03:17.555
Right?

00:03:17.555 --> 00:03:20.580
Douglas Hofstetler is a philosopher.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:25.240
And what he does is he replaces in
a satire male-based generic words

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:27.040
with race-based ones.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:30.270
Mankind becomes whitekind,

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:34.870
freshman becomes freshwhite,
chairman becomes chairwhite.

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:40.030
And in this imaginary world, it becomes
pretty ridiculous that we would ever

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:46.080
expect people of color to feel included by
these terms, or that these terms wouldn't

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:51.400
have real and lasting consequences, things
that reflect are pretty gruesome history.

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:54.510
But that's exactly what we do.

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:58.680
It's what we do to women,
and it's what I did to Ella.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:02.300
Of course, some women, and

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:06.730
many women do feel included by these
terms, and that's entirely understandable.

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:09.870
Women who become congressmen for

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:13.790
example, work incredibly
hard to get where they are.

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:15.620
They overcome enormous obstacles.

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:16.906
They earn the title.

00:04:18.856 --> 00:04:23.332
When women don't feel included,
I've heard them speak up and

00:04:23.332 --> 00:04:25.790
then I've watched the eye rolls.

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:31.849
I've listened as they get
labeled high maintenance,

00:04:31.849 --> 00:04:34.760
overly sensitive, or much worse.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:43.150
When we use female words to insult women,

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:47.280
we communicate a pretty disturbing
message, and we communicate the same

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:52.670
disturbing message when we use only
positive words with male terms.

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:57.600
Sociologist Sheryl Clyman
explains that when you use

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:03.170
chairman for women, yes,
a particular woman can become a chairman.

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:05.930
But women as a group cannot.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:09.340
Chairman sets expectation
that chairmen are men.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:14.460
And when a woman achieves that title,
she's merely an exception.

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:18.110
Never the rule.

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:20.529
Rarely the expectation.

00:05:20.529 --> 00:05:26.871
When we use male based generics,
we erase women linguistically.

00:05:26.871 --> 00:05:29.288
Symbolically, annihilate them.

00:05:31.076 --> 00:05:35.475
Now, there's a visionary who I love,
Marilyn Frye, and she imagines all of

00:05:35.475 --> 00:05:40.310
this gender inequity as essentially
a birdcage that traps women inside.

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:42.180
There are many, many wires to the cage.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:44.020
One wire is the wage gap.

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:46.160
Another wire is sexual harassment.

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:48.300
Another one is men's
violence against women.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:49.760
And there's sexist English.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:53.800
The point here is that there are many,
many wires.

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:59.270
And these wires,
no wire by itself traps the bird inside.

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:04.060
It's all of the wires working together
in combination that creates a prison.

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:08.439
We can break that prison.

00:06:08.439 --> 00:06:12.725
With so many wires I often get
asked why focus on language.

00:06:12.725 --> 00:06:16.420
Aren't there other wires
that are more important?

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:20.310
Let me be clear, every wire is important.

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:26.580
Every wire deserves to be broken and every
wire that we break creates a new opening.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:30.600
I like to single out sexist language for
three reasons.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:35.060
The first reason is that everyone
has the power to break this wire.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:37.260
It's entirely within our control.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:39.660
Each of us decides constantly
what words we're going to say,

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:42.600
what we're going to type in
a text message, put in an email.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:44.665
What language displays on our websites or

00:06:44.665 --> 00:06:47.320
goes in a official publications
that we put up there.

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:52.330
Language is a choice
that we make constantly.

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:53.070
It's our choice.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:58.810
The second reason is that we
can break this wire right now,

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:00.670
you don't have to wait.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:01.190
It's so

00:07:01.190 --> 00:07:07.540
much easier to start saying you all today
than it is to close the wage gap tomorrow.

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:08.780
Though, of course, we should do that, too.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:13.610
The third reason is that
gender inclusive language

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:16.240
is inclusive of everyone, even men.

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:22.690
It doesn't harm anyone when
you switch to inclusive terms.

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:26.620
Instead, it proactively signals
your commitment to women.

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:29.686
And what more powerful message to send.

00:07:31.218 --> 00:07:34.849
I want to acknowledge that while
this solution is really simple,

00:07:34.849 --> 00:07:36.470
that doesn't make it easy.

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:40.230
Male dominated words are everywhere.

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:42.760
They're deeply embedded in our culture.

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:47.180
Getting rid of them requires intention,
it requires unlearning.

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:51.320
It requires a community to
hold one another accountable.

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:52.610
So let's do this together.

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:59.160
Let's stop using gendered words and
replace them with gender neutral options.

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:02.240
Freshman becomes first year student.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:04.260
Chairman becomes chair.

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:10.160
Arbuckle fellow becomes Arbuckle coach or
scholar or leader.

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:13.080
You guys becomes you all.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:15.825
Or if you're like me from the South,
y'all.

00:08:15.825 --> 00:08:20.470
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Language is a tool of thought.

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:25.750
It's a tool for building community, and
it's a tool for breaking birdcages.

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:30.670
Each of us has the power to create
the world that we want to be in.

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:34.800
So do we want a language that
reflects our history of excluding and

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:36.330
diminishing women?

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:37.390
No.

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:42.280
Do we prefer a language that aspires
towards something much more inclusive for

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:46.520
our future, for our mothers,
our sisters, our daughters, our nieces?

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:52.810
Your decision to become a gender
savvy speaker is a decision

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:57.336
to build this world,
a world that takes women seriously.

00:08:57.336 --> 00:09:02.280
Sheryl Clyman teaches that inclusive

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:07.510
language is the resource that each
of us has at the tip of our tongues.

00:09:07.510 --> 00:09:11.146
Let's start tasting its freedom today.

00:09:11.146 --> 00:09:13.648
Thank you.

00:09:13.648 --> 00:09:16.002
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:16.002 --> 00:09:20.199
[MUSIC]

